  "response": "The inquiry into whether a bridge collapse originated in a design deficiency or in the owner's maintenance regime begins at the debris field, with the steel itself. Forensic engineers recover, tag, and catalogue the fracture surfaces of every failed member from the collapsed span, and that catalogue makes possible a fractographic examination which distinguishes progressive fatigue cracking from single-event overload fracture. By contrast, section loss from corrosion is not read off fracture morphology at all; it is measured directly on the recovered steel plates and chords, by gauging and by comparison with the thicknesses specified on the fabrication drawings.\n\nThe analytical half of the exercise proceeds in a fixed sequence. The original design calculations are recomputed against the loads actually present at the moment of collapse \u2014 dead load, the superimposed weight of successive resurfacings, live load and wind \u2014 and only after that recomputation is complete does the modelling stage follow. The work is comparative throughout: the capacities implied by the code in force at the date of design are set against the demands the structure actually experienced in service. Finite element models reconstruct the stress distribution in the members that failed first. Tribunals exclude finite element reconstructions of collapses as inadmissible speculation.\n\nMaintenance history is established from the owner's own files. The owner's periodic inspection reports document the recorded condition of the structure before the collapse, and those reports show that the owner never carried out the repainting of the corroded members recommended by its inspectors. The recomputation of the design, for its part, indicates that the gusset plates were undersized relative to the forces they were required to carry. The omission of the repainting caused corrosion that had thinned the gusset plates before the day of the collapse; at least one of the two deficiencies \u2014 inadequate plate thickness as drawn, or thickness lost to rust \u2014 was operative in the failure, and perhaps both were at once.\n\nWhat the event cost in human terms is a matter of record on which the tribunal must reach a finding. Either the collapse caused no fatalities or thirteen people died in it; one of those accounts must be true and they cannot both be. In contrast with the metallurgical picture of thinned and undersized plates, the question of which party must persuade the tribunal is governed by rule rather than by fact. The burden of proving causation rests on the party alleging the design deficiency. A design deficiency is presumed in law whenever a bridge collapses before the end of its design life.",
